---
# What CrowdSec reads on this box. Written by bootstrap.sh — edit here, in git,
# and re-run it.
#
# One file, one source per document. Every public listener on this VPS appears
# exactly once, and the `type` is what selects the parser: get it wrong and the
# lines are read, parsed by nothing, and matched by no scenario — a silent
# no-op that looks identical to "no attacks yet".
#
# THIS BOX IS THE BLIND SPOT THIS FIXES. Its lanes bypass Cloudflare and Traefik
# entirely, so none of this traffic reaches the cluster's access log, its rate
# limiter, or infra/crowdsec. What is not read here is not seen anywhere.

# sshd, from journald rather than /var/log/auth.log. Ubuntu still writes the
# file, but journald is the source of truth and survives the day someone turns
# rsyslog off — and `journalctl_filter` costs nothing when the file also exists.
#
# Note this only ever sees the PUBLIC sshd. Tailscale SSH is handled inside
# tailscaled and never touches sshd's logs, which is correct: those sessions are
# already authenticated by the tailnet and must not be bannable from here.
source: journalctl
journalctl_filter:
  - "_SYSTEMD_UNIT=ssh.service"
labels:
  type: syslog
---
# Caddy — the media lane (Jellyfin). JSON access logs, which is what the caddy
# parser expects; the Caddyfile must keep `log { format json }` for this to
# parse, and bootstrap.sh renders it that way.
#
# This is the busiest and most exposed surface on the box: a public HTTPS
# service whose only other control is Jellyfin's own login page.
source: file
filenames:
  - /var/log/caddy/access.log
labels:
  type: caddy
---
# haproxy — the Minecraft relay. There is no CrowdSec collection for Minecraft
# itself (the protocol is not HTTP and nobody has written scenarios for it), so
# what this buys is narrower than the Caddy lane: connection-level noise, not
# gameplay abuse.
#
# It is here anyway because it is free and because the haproxy stick-table
# rejections show up in this log — so a source that trips the connection limit
# repeatedly becomes visible as a pattern rather than a counter nobody reads.
source: file
filenames:
  - /var/log/haproxy.log
labels:
  type: haproxy
---
# squid — the egress proxy. Reachable only over the tailnet (or from the house,
# if PUBLIC_FALLBACK=true), so this is not an attack surface in the way the
# others are.
#
# It is acquired for a different reason: this log is where a STOLEN PROXY
# CREDENTIAL would show up, as authenticated requests from somewhere that is not
# the cluster. Nothing bans on it today — there is no stock scenario for "this
# proxy user appeared from a new address" — but the lines have to be being read
# before any such scenario could ever fire.
source: file
filenames:
  - /var/log/squid/access.log
labels:
  type: syslog
